{"context": "A research team needed to run experiments on language model behavior using prompts about topics sensitive to the Chinese government. The models under study were open-weight models that multiple third-party hosts could serve, so the team had to choose which hosting providers would handle the requests. Hosts differed in headquarters location, data retention and logging terms, content filtering behavior, latency, pricing, and availability for the required models. The team knew that the choice of host could affect how sensitive prompts and outputs were stored or processed, and whether results would reflect the model itself or host-side filtering. The team had limited information about the data-handling practices of some hosts, and the same model weights were available through more than one route. The team had to settle on a single provider arrangement before starting the full experimental run.", "options": ["Run all experiments through one non-Chinese hosting provider accessed via an API router.", "Run all experiments through hosting providers headquartered in mainland China."], "principles": ["Prefers treating test-recognition as a first-class threat over assuming behavioural tests are reliable", "Prefers goal plus incentive inside a realistic role-play over explicit instructions to misbehave", "Prefers matched controls (incentive removed, benign intent, secure code, single-trait) over a lone headline effect", "Prefers indirect probes of eval-awareness over asking \"is this an evaluation?\"", "Prefers learning the model's own concept of eval vs deployment over researcher-chosen instances", "Prefers neutral, innocent-looking prompts and covert harm over overtly malicious content", "Prefers testing rival explanations (post-hoc blame, spurious correlates, positional bias, artifacts) before accepting a result", "Prefers out-of-distribution and held-out checks over in-distribution accuracy", "Prefers asking whether a false-belief intervention survives little or no CoT over whether it merely seems plausible"], "persona": "", "past_choices": []}
